  "behavior": [
    "Index-based adapters express native Append(array[index]) and Append(std::move(array[index])) against actual internal storage without overlapping Rust container borrows. No unsafe production code was added.",
    "Every append path shares one staging engine: allocate target block first, invoke constructor against stable source storage, write result and publish size only after success.",
    "Internal donors remain in their original slots. Move construction can mutate them before throwing; logical size stays unchanged and the newly allocated target block is retained for retry.",
    "14 native rows cover same-block and different-block sources, target allocation, full descriptor-table growth, copy and move constructor throws, stable source addresses, successful returned references and retry without repeated allocation.",
    "Native probe uses -fno-access-control against unchanged pinned headers to assert descriptor capacity2 before and4 after each table-growth scenario. Rust unit verifies capacity2-to4 and both original payload-block addresses.",
    "Signed, end, huge and empty source indices are rejected before allocation or callbacks as a checked Rust extension; native invalid source references are not exercised.",
    "A non-Clone/non-Default payload exercises both constructors.17 new integration cases and1 new unit test extend previous coverage; earlier40 native rows remain byte-identical."
  ],
  "limitations": [
    "Only Append internal-source domains are covered. Internal source aliasing in SetValue and insertion remains open.",
    "Explicit constructor callbacks select native copy/move behavior; automatic C++ capability selection, address-dependent in-place construction and exception/noexcept ABI are not inferred.",
    "Proof covers initialized logical source slots. Initialized hidden Rust tail values and invalid native source references are not claimed.",
    "Descriptor byte-layout and other generic ABI gaps remain; pointer-address and capacity observations are scoped to tested native/Rust configurations.",
    "Separately observed EraseLast count update currently precedes Rust Drop while native decrements after destruction; metadata-observer behavior needs a dedicated follow-up and is not changed here."
  ],
